    18	## [1.3.0] - 2026-08-04
    19	
    20	Night-mode + doctrine + release-engineering train (PLAN-162/165
    21	ceremony 2; PLAN-166/167/168 release-hold closure and install/upgrade
    22	ownership; PLAN-177 GA re-pass cures; PLAN-178 spawn acceptance
    23	contract v2, native cost cross-check and vacuity lint). The headline
    24	feature is night-mode; one cross-rail security P0
    25	closed, one published-contract conflict settled by ADR instead of by
    26	silence, and the install/upgrade ownership decision collapsed into a
    27	single audited function. As always: governance and auditability — no
    28	speed claim.
    29	
    30	### Added — night-mode: Owner-invoked autonomy posture toggle (PLAN-165, ADR-185)
    31	
    32	- **`/night-mode` command + `.claude/scripts/night-mode.py`**: the Owner
    33	  flips the per-machine autonomy posture (`permissions.defaultMode`
    34	  overlay in the gitignored `.claude/settings.local.json`) with
    35	  next-session semantics — "arm before sleep, autonomous overnight
    36	  session, disarm in the morning". The tracked `settings.json` posture is
    37	  never touched; templates and adopter defaults are unchanged.
    38	- **Arming autonomy is a HUMAN action** (Owner-ratified OQ1-redo): the
    39	  toggle refuses under CI; the writer script carries a self-path guard
    40	  and the Bash rail carries a best-effort invocation matcher (defense in
    41	  depth — the real boundary is the script's own guard plus the session
    42	  deny surface, and the docs say exactly that, not more).
    43	- **Audited**: every `on`/`off`/refusal emits `night_mode_toggled` into
    44	  the HMAC chain (schema row in `SPEC/v1/audit-log.schema.md`); live-fire
    45	  proven — toggle events and a negative-control `refused` line verified
    46	  on-chain before this release.
    47	- `bypassPermissions` was deliberately CUT from the design: it would trip
    48	  the `settings_tamper_tripwires` boot check by construction. The honest
    49	  escape valve stays `claude --permission-mode bypassPermissions` (one
    50	  session, explicit, ephemeral).
    51	
    52	### Security — sentinel unlock now requires provenance inside git worktrees (ADR-119 Invariant 5)
    53	
    54	- **Adopter-visible tightening of the recovery contract** (SPEC/v1
    55	  `sentinel-format.schema.md` §unlock): inside a git work tree, the
    56	  `CEO_SENTINEL_UNLOCK` + `CEO_SENTINEL_UNLOCK_ACK` pair alone no longer
    57	  grants — you must also provide provenance via
    58	  `CEO_SESSION_ANCHOR_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD)` (captured BEFORE the
    59	  session) **or** `CEO_SENTINEL_UNLOCK_SHA256=<sha256 of approved.md>`.
    60	  The block message teaches both forms when a window is armed. This
    61	  closes a real escalation: during an unlock window the agent could
    62	  author its own `approved.md` (a path guarded by neither rail) and
    63	  self-grant. Classified MINOR under this repo's published policy
    64	  (`VERSIONING.md` §MINOR: "a new trust boundary adopters must
    65	  understand"); MAJOR is reserved for schema-consumer breakage
    66	  (removed/renamed fields, dropped event types), which this is not.
    67	  **Action needed only if you scripted the 2-var unlock inside a git
    68	  worktree** — add one of the two exports above.
    69	
    70	### Fixed — case-fold bypass on canonical AND kernel rails (PLAN-162 W2, P0)
    71	
    72	- On case-insensitive filesystems (APFS default), `fnmatch.fnmatchcase`
    73	  let `.claude/settings.JSON` / `.claude/hooks/_lib/audit_emit.PY` slip
    74	  past BOTH guard rails while the write lands on the real file. Both
    75	  rails now case-fold before matching. Found first-hand during the
    76	  PLAN-162 debate; verified by red-first tests on both rails.
    77	
    78	### Changed — hook-deadline doctrine settled by ADR-186 (fail-CLOSED)
    79	
    80	- The canonical-edit matcher now enforces a **per-invocation wall
    81	  deadline that fails CLOSED** (`_HOOK_WALL_BUDGET_S`, injectable clock),
    82	  replacing the unbounded sentinel sweep — and the published
    83	  fail-open-on-infrastructure contract in `CLAUDE.md` §4 / `AGENTS.md` §1
    84	  now names this exception explicitly: a timeout *inside the matcher* is
    85	  an incomplete verification, not infrastructure. Recovery route:
    86	  provenance-pinned unlock (`CEO_SENTINEL_UNLOCK` +
    87	  `CEO_SESSION_ANCHOR_SHA` or `CEO_SENTINEL_UNLOCK_SHA256`).
    88	- Sentinel-verification **cache partitioned** (signature validity no
    89	  longer keyed per-target). This closes the measured saturation window —
    90	  4.16 s consumed of a 5 s budget at 20 candidate paths — that made the
    91	  old fail-open deadline attacker-reachable by planting sentinels
    92	  (ADR-164-AMEND-1). A correctness/security fix; no throughput claim.
    93	- Deadline blocks are **countable in the chain**: the veto breadcrumb
    94	  carries `reason_code=canonical_edit_hook_fault` instead of
    95	  masquerading as a missing-sentinel block.
    96	
    97	### Changed — pair-rail recalibrated 120/150 → 180/210 (ADR-110-AMEND-2)
    98	
    99	- Internal cap 180 s under a 210 s registration, ratified only after a
   100	  live substrate probe proved the harness honors a 210 s hook
   101	  registration (evidence committed:
   102	  `.claude/plans/PLAN-162/probe-210s-GO-EVIDENCE.md`).
   103	- `pair_rail_case` events now carry `timeout_ms` (int — a float in an
   104	  HMAC-covered field silently drops the whole event), and the §3
   105	  escalation trigger is the **censoring rate**, not p95 (the p95 of a
   106	  censored sample is inestimable).
   107	
   108	### Fixed — scheduled workflows that were red without surfacing in push CI
   109	
   110	- **mutation-gate**: kill-rate now parsed from mutmut's junitxml (the old
   111	  regex parser NEVER reported a rate — historical "96.7%" came from an
   112	  inflated formula), artifact redaction inlined and fail-closed, and the
   113	  `actions/checkout` SHA re-pinned (which also cures the
   114	  supply-chain-watch drift red present since 2026-07-20).
   115	- **tournament**: stderr banner no longer merged into the cost-projection
   116	  JSON (`2>&1` unmerge).
   117	- **reality-ledger**: required labels created idempotently
   118	  (`gh label create --force`) so fresh installs cannot red on a missing
   119	  label.
   120	- **ceo-boot**: 24th Tier-S check `scheduled_workflows_red` closes the
   121	  "scheduled gate red for weeks, invisible" class — with cure-detection
   122	  (a red scheduled lane whose newest completed run across ALL trigger
   123	  events is green reports `cured_pending_cron`, not red).
   124	
   125	### Changed — install/upgrade semantics (PLAN-166/167/168)
   126	
   127	Adopter-visible behavior of `scripts/install.sh` / `scripts/upgrade.sh`
   128	changed in this release — what a v1.2.0 adopter notices when upgrading:
   129	
   130	- **Ownership is ONE decision, not a cascade (ADR-190).** On upgrade,
   131	  whether the framework owns `PROTOCOL.md`, `SPEC/v1` or
   132	  `.claude/.framework-version` is answered by a single pure decision
   133	  function (`_ownership_verdict()` in
   134	  `scripts/_framework_manifest_set.sh`) over the observed ownership
   135	  dimensions — execution-state faults (e.g. a failed backup) stay
   136	  caller-side by design; `upgrade.sh` observes → calls → executes
   137	  instead of deciding locally. Fresh installs sit BEFORE the decision:
   138	  they record the registered delivery (the ADR-155 baseline manifest)
   139	  that upgrade's observations later read. Contract:
   140	  `docs/ownership-decision-table.md`.
   141	- **`SPEC/v1` is a forced route on upgrade.** A framework-owned
   142	  `SPEC/v1` is backed up to `.claude.bak/<timestamp>/SPEC/v1` and
   143	  replaced wholesale — a local edit is a fork of the published
   144	  compliance contract, not a customization. A pre-existing `SPEC/v1`
   145	  with no delivery record is byte-compared against the pristine SPECs
   146	  shipped at v1.2.0 and earlier: a match refreshes it, anything else is
   147	  preserved in place with a named WARNING (ADR-155-AMEND-1). Skipped on
   148	  `--ceremony user` installs.
   149	- **`.claude/.framework-version` is the version marker.** Written on
   150	  install and refreshed on framework-owned, unskipped upgrades;
   151	  `check-framework-updates.sh` and forensic triage read it MARKER-FIRST
   152	  — a well-formed marker validated against its delivery record is the
   153	  signal; an absent, unrecorded, malformed or integrity-failed marker
   154	  falls back to root `VERSION`. The adopter repo's root `VERSION` file
   155	  is deliberately never touched by upgrade — after a framework-owned
   156	  upgrade, the validated marker (not root `VERSION`) is what carries
   157	  the installed framework version.
   158	- **`PROTOCOL.md` pointer has ONE generator.** Install and upgrade
   159	  render the pointer through the same shared generator (byte-identical
   160	  output on both paths); a degraded pointer body is cured with a backup
   161	  and adopter edits are preserved. Skipped on `--ceremony user`
   162	  installs, which never create root files.
   163	
   164	### Fixed — release-verdict readers share ONE fail-closed grammar (PLAN-177)
   165	
   166	The GA re-pass over rc.3 ended NO-GO; the cures landed inside this
   167	train (rc.4) instead of being deferred:
   168	
   169	- **Both release decision gates** — the server-side
   170	  `validate-pair-rail-verdict.py` (step 15) and the local
   171	  `_release_tag_guard.py` (all-modes enforcement) — now parse the
   172	  signed verdict with the same strict ASCII/YAML grammar: indented
   173	  continuations of a scalar, comments glued to the value, Unicode
   174	  whitespace stuck to the token (`GO<U+00A0>` is NOT `GO`) and
   175	  separator-less keys (`verdict:GO`) are each a NAMED rejection, never
   176	  a silent normalization into the authorizing token. Proven by
   177	  cross-reader probe fixtures that run the two rails on every key
   178	  shape and require identical answers.
   179	- **Gitignore delivery is symlink-safe**: the three writers refuse to
   180	  follow a symlinked `.gitignore`/`.claude/.gitignore`, dry-run
   181	  previews print what WOULD be appended (never "asserted clean" over
   182	  debris), and the root-gitignore symlink guard is part of the
   183	  baseline enumeration.
   184	- **plans/ schema docs refresh is hash-gated on upgrade**: only a
   185	  byte-pristine copy of a KNOWN prior framework generation of
   186	  `PLAN-SCHEMA.md`/`DEBATE-SCHEMA.md` is replaced (with backup);
   187	  an adopter-modified schema is PRESERVED loudly. Closes the F3 STALE
   188	  signature the parity e2e flagged.
